The leak is only observable in the directory [[mkdtemp]] chose, so
the test chooses it: pointing [[tempfile.tempdir]] at a directory of
our own makes \enquote{nothing was left behind} a statement about
one empty directory rather than about a [[/tmp]] shared with every
other process on the machine.
We interrupt the [[git clone]] specifically---the [[git bundle
list-heads]] before it must still run, or the branch would fail
before it ever created the directory whose fate we are testing.
The [[pytest.raises]] is half the assertion: cleaning up is only
correct if the interruption still gets through, and a handler that
swallowed it would leave no temporary directory either.

<<test functions>>=
def test_resolve_source_bundle_cleans_up_on_interrupt(
    workdir, monkeypatch,
):
    """Ctrl-C during unbundling leaves no temporary directory."""
    import tempfile
    from learnlog import cli

    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    bundle_path = workdir / "test.bundle"
    repo.git(
        "bundle", "create", str(bundle_path), "--all"
    )

    scratch = workdir.parent / "resolve-source-tmp"
    scratch.mkdir()
    monkeypatch.setattr(tempfile, "tempdir", str(scratch))

    real_run = subprocess.run

    def interrupt_the_clone(cmd, *args, **kwargs):
        if "clone" in cmd:
            raise KeyboardInterrupt
        return real_run(cmd, *args, **kwargs)

    monkeypatch.setattr(
        cli.subprocess, "run", interrupt_the_clone
    )

    with pytest.raises(KeyboardInterrupt):
        cli.resolve_source(str(bundle_path))

    assert list(scratch.glob("learnlog-play-*")) == []
