\section{Setting up a project for learnlog}
\label{sec:setup}

The [[init]] command (\cref{sec:init}) is what a student
runs once per project to turn an ordinary working directory
into a learnlog-enabled one.  Three independent things need
to happen for learnlog to record reliably, and [[init]]
handles all of them: the [[.learnlog/]] repository must
exist at the project \emph{root}, a Python environment with
[[learnlog]] installed must be available to run student
code, and that environment must be wired up so that
[[learnlog]] activates \emph{before} the student's script
is compiled---otherwise a [[SyntaxError]] in [[main.py]]
passes through silently because the explicit
[[import learnlog]] line is never reached.

The repository placement matters because [[learnlog]]
initialises itself lazily on first import: if no
[[.learnlog/]] is found walking upward from the current
directory (\cref{learnlog}), a fresh one is created in
whatever the current directory happens to be.  A student
who runs their first script from a subdirectory therefore
ends up with [[.learnlog/]] tangled into that
subdirectory---tangled into [[src/]] or [[lab1/]] rather
than the project root.  Creating the repository explicitly
up front is the simplest way to eliminate that race.

The Python-environment setup always creates a project-local
venv using the PyPI [[virtualenv]] package rather than the
stdlib [[venv]] module.  The two packages are nearly
interchangeable from the caller's perspective, but
[[virtualenv]] bundles its own [[pip]] wheels---so it works
even on Debian/Ubuntu systems where [[python3-venv]] is a
separate APT package that has not been installed, and on
PEP 668-marked Pythons where [[pip install]] against the
system interpreter is rejected outright.  We have
[[virtualenv]] as a runtime dependency anyway, so having a
single code path is cheaper than maintaining a second one
that tries stdlib [[venv]] first.  The older [[pip install
--user]] fallback is gone: it required probing PEP 668
state on the target interpreter, produced a different
layout for logging (no venv means no [[.pth]] anchor), and
broke down on the systems we most cared about supporting.

The autostart wiring is a single [[.pth]] file and a small
text marker placed inside the venv's [[site-packages]].
[[site.py]] processes [[.pth]] files at interpreter startup,
before the main script is compiled, so the [[sys.excepthook]]
that [[learnlog.initialize()]] installs is in place in time
to see top-level [[SyntaxError]]s.  The marker file records
the project root so that scripts run from outside the
project (for example [[python /tmp/scratch.py]] inside an
activated venv) still commit to the right
[[.learnlog/]].  Both files are written by
[[write_autostart_files]]
(\cref{sec:setup-autostart-hook}).

The helpers that support [[init]] are split across the
following subsections by concern: bounding where a project
may be created at all, choosing a language,
finding the interpreter, locating [[pip]] and [[python]]
once a venv exists, writing the autostart hook, and
finally the pipeline that wires them together.


\subsection{Refusing a home-directory project root}
\label{sec:setup-home-guard}

Repository \emph{discovery} is bounded: the upward walk in
[[find_learnlog_dir]] stops before the home directory, so no
misplaced script can make learnlog adopt [[$HOME]] by accident
(\cref{learnlog}).  [[init]] is the other way a repository comes
into existence, and it is the more dangerous one, because
discovery never runs afterwards to correct it.  A student who
types [[learnlog init python]] one directory too high---in
[[$HOME]] itself---gets a [[.learnlog/]] there \emph{and} a
marker file naming it, and the marker is authoritative: every
later run of that venv reads it and stages the whole home
directory, from [[.ssh/]] to the browser cache, into the log.
Nothing in the run path can second-guess it, since the marker's
whole purpose is to override the guess.

So [[init]] applies discovery's own predicate to the root it is
about to create.  Importing [[_is_home_or_above]] rather than
restating the rule is what keeps the two entry points from
drifting apart: whatever counts as \enquote{too high} for the
walk counts as too high for [[init]] by construction.

Only that one condition refuses.  A project directory that is
also a Git checkout is the documented normal layout---the log
repository is deliberately hidden in [[.learnlog/]] precisely so
it can live alongside the student's own [[.git/]]---so the Git
boundary that bounds the \emph{walk} has no business bounding
[[init]].

The refusal is nevertheless overridable.  \enquote{Never} would
be a guess about the user's intent, and there are honest reasons
to want a log rooted at a directory we would otherwise refuse: a
teacher recording a demo in a scratch account, or an
investigation into learnlog's own behaviour in that layout.  What
the guard has to prevent is the \emph{accident}, and a flag the
user has to type does that: [[--force]] costs one word when it is
meant, and is never typed by the student who merely forgot to
[[cd]].

The caller supplies the way out as a hint, because it differs by
command---[[init]] can offer [[--force]], while [[doctor]] can
only send the user somewhere else (\cref{sec:doctor-command}).
Keeping the \emph{reason} in one place and letting the next step
vary is what makes the helper worth having at all.

<<helper functions>>=
def refuse_home_root(path, hint):
    """Refuse ``path`` as a project root if it is $HOME or above.

    Raises ``SystemExit`` with an explanation followed by
    ``hint``, which should tell the caller how to proceed.
    Returns ``None`` for any acceptable path.
    """
    if not _is_home_or_above(path):
        return
    raise SystemExit(
        f"Refusing to make {path} a learnlog project root: it "
        f"is your home directory or an ancestor of it, so every "
        f"run would commit unrelated files into the log.  {hint}"
    )
@

