\subsection{Resolving the target Python}
\label{sec:setup-target-python}

Students most commonly install [[learnlog]] with
[[pipx install learnlog]], which places the tool inside a
private virtual environment under
[[~/.local/share/pipx/venvs/learnlog/]].  By the time any
[[init]] code runs, [[sys.executable]] already points at
that private interpreter---\emph{not} at the Python the
student will actually use for coursework.

The same separation applies to [[uv tool install]] when the
tool lives in uv's managed tools directory.  In particular,
[[uv tool install --python 3.10 learnlog]] is the supported
escape hatch for machines whose system Python is still 3.9
after [[learnlog]] itself moves to Python~3.10.

That distinction matters for two separate reasons.  First,
using [[sys.executable]] to create [[.venv/]] would bind
the student's project to pipx's hidden interpreter, which
is not under the student's control and may be upgraded or
removed when pipx is.  Second, asking pipx's interpreter
about PEP 668 always reports \enquote{not managed}
regardless of the host distro, because virtual environments
never carry the [[EXTERNALLY-MANAGED]] marker; the student's
system Python may be externally managed even when pipx's is
not.

Picking a target interpreter by name avoids both problems.
The default [[python3]] matches what almost every student
types to run scripts; the fallback to [[python]] covers
older systems where [[python3]] is unlinked; the
[[--python]] option lets a user pin to an exact version
like [[python3.12]].

<<helper functions>>=
def resolve_target_python(explicit=None):
    """Return the student's Python interpreter as a path.

    If ``explicit`` is given (either a name on ``PATH``
    like ``"python3.12"`` or a full path), it is resolved
    via :func:`shutil.which`.  Otherwise ``python3`` is
    tried first, then ``python``.  Raises ``SystemExit``
    with a clear message if nothing resolves.
    """
    candidates = [explicit] if explicit else ["python3", "python"]
    for name in candidates:
        found = shutil.which(name)
        if found:
            return pathlib.Path(found)
    raise SystemExit(
        f"Cannot find a Python interpreter (tried "
        f"{', '.join(candidates)}).  Pass --python to "
        f"specify one explicitly."
    )
@

Let's verify the fallback order and the error path.  The
tests patch [[shutil.which]] so they do not depend on what
interpreters happen to be installed on the host.

<<test functions>>=
def test_resolve_target_python_uses_explicit(monkeypatch):
    """An explicit value is resolved via shutil.which."""
    from learnlog.cli import resolve_target_python
    monkeypatch.setattr(
        "shutil.which",
        lambda name: (
            "/opt/py/python3.12"
            if name == "python3.12"
            else None
        ),
    )
    result = resolve_target_python("python3.12")
    assert result == pathlib.Path("/opt/py/python3.12")

def test_resolve_target_python_prefers_python3(monkeypatch):
    """With no argument, python3 is tried before python."""
    from learnlog.cli import resolve_target_python
    monkeypatch.setattr(
        "shutil.which",
        lambda name: (
            "/usr/bin/python3"
            if name == "python3"
            else None
        ),
    )
    assert resolve_target_python() == pathlib.Path(
        "/usr/bin/python3"
    )

def test_resolve_target_python_errors_when_missing(monkeypatch):
    """SystemExit with a helpful message when nothing found."""
    from learnlog.cli import resolve_target_python
    monkeypatch.setattr("shutil.which", lambda name: None)
    with pytest.raises(SystemExit, match="Cannot find"):
        resolve_target_python()
