\subsection{Emitting deactivation code for [[bash]]}
\label{sec:emit-deactivation}

Deactivation is intentionally more conservative than activation.
Calling [[deactivate]] blindly would tear down whichever venv happens
to be active in the shell, even if it has nothing to do with the
current learnlog project.  Instead we emit a guard that compares the
current [[VIRTUAL_ENV]] against the resolved project [[.venv/]] and
only then calls the shell function installed by venv activation.

That means [[learnlog deactivate]] is meant to be used after
[[learnlog activate]] (or after an equivalent manual activation of the
same project [[.venv/]]).  If no matching venv is active, the emitted
code becomes a no-op.

<<helper functions>>=
def emit_deactivate_shell_code(venv_dir, shell):
    """Return shell code that deactivates ``venv_dir`` if active."""
    quoted_venv = shell_quote(venv_dir, shell)
    if shell is Shell.bash:
        return (
            f'if [ "${{VIRTUAL_ENV:-}}" = {quoted_venv} ] '
            f'&& declare -F deactivate >/dev/null; then deactivate; fi'
        )
    raise AssertionError(f"Unsupported shell: {shell}")
@

<<test functions>>=
def test_emit_deactivate_shell_code_bash_guards_venv(workdir):
    """Bash deactivation only targets the matching project venv."""
    from learnlog.cli import Shell, emit_deactivate_shell_code
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    venv_dir = (workdir / ".venv").resolve()
    assert emit_deactivate_shell_code(venv_dir, Shell.bash) == (
        "if [ \"${VIRTUAL_ENV:-}\" = "
        f"{shlex.quote(str(venv_dir))}"
        " ] && declare -F deactivate >/dev/null; then deactivate; fi"
    )
@


\subsection{Activation hint for a fresh venv}
\label{sec:setup-activation-hint}

When [[init_python]] creates a venv, the student still has to
activate it before running [[python]] or [[pytest]] in the
same shell---[[init]] cannot do that for them, because a
child process cannot mutate its parent shell's environment.
A one-line hint printed after creation is therefore the best
we can do.

The hint differs by platform because the activation scripts
do: POSIX shells expect [[source .venv/bin/activate]],
whereas Windows venvs place the scripts in [[Scripts\]] and
have no [[source]] builtin (the student types the path to the
activator directly, and both [[cmd.exe]] and PowerShell pick
up the right extension via [[PATHEXT]]).  This is the same
POSIX/Windows layout split that [[find_in_venv]] already
branches on in \cref{sec:setup-pip-in-venv}, so factoring it
into its own helper keeps the two concerns in sync---if
Windows ever drops [[Scripts\]], both helpers fail together
and the fix is localised.

We branch on [[sys.platform]] rather than [[os.name]]
because [[pathlib]] inspects [[os.name]] at [[Path]]
construction time to pick POSIX vs. Windows semantics.
A test that monkeypatched [[os.name]] to [[nt]] would
silently switch every [[Path]] created afterwards---including
paths pytest builds internally for its own error
reporting---to Windows flavour, and the first backslash in a
rendered path triggers a [[ValueError]] deep inside pytest's
terminal writer.  [[sys.platform]] has no such side effect.

<<helper functions>>=
def venv_activation_hint(venv_dir):
    """Return a shell-appropriate activation hint for ``venv_dir``.

    Used by :func:`init_python` to nudge the student after a
    fresh venv is created.  POSIX shells need ``source
    .../bin/activate``; Windows shells run
    ``...\\Scripts\\activate`` directly.
    """
    if sys.platform.startswith("win"):
        return f"Next step: {venv_dir}\\Scripts\\activate"
    return f"Next step: source {venv_dir}/bin/activate"
@

We verify both branches by monkeypatching [[sys.platform]] so
the test is independent of the host platform and passes on
every CI runner.

<<test functions>>=
def test_venv_activation_hint_posix(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
    """POSIX hint uses ``source .../bin/activate``."""
    from learnlog.cli import venv_activation_hint
    monkeypatch.setattr("sys.platform", "linux")
    venv = tmp_path / ".venv"
    assert venv_activation_hint(venv) == (
        f"Next step: source {venv}/bin/activate"
    )

def test_venv_activation_hint_windows(monkeypatch, tmp_path):
    """Windows hint uses ``...\\Scripts\\activate``."""
    from learnlog.cli import venv_activation_hint
    monkeypatch.setattr("sys.platform", "win32")
    venv = tmp_path / ".venv"
    assert venv_activation_hint(venv) == (
        f"Next step: {venv}\\Scripts\\activate"
    )
