\subsection{The [[tutorial]] command}
\label{sec:tutorial}

The tutorial subcommand delegates to [[pytorial]] rather than
reimplementing a second step engine inside [[learnlog]].
That keeps the host CLI focused on learnlog's own behavior while
still letting us ship guided walkthroughs for common tasks such as
project setup, playback, sharing, and ProgSnap2 analysis.

We mount pytorial's embedded command tree under [[learnlog
tutorial]], which gives us [[list]], [[run]], and [[review]]
without the standalone [[install]] command that only makes sense
for pytorial's top-level CLI.
Passing [[namespace="learnlog"]] keeps pytorial's persisted state
isolated under a learnlog-specific directory instead of mixing it
with other embedded hosts on the same machine.
The builtin [[using-tutorials]] self-tour is prepended
automatically, and the rest of the catalog comes from the Markdown
tutorials we ship as package data under [[learnlog/tutorials/]].
We list those tutorial files explicitly so [[learnlog tutorial
list]] follows the intended student-to-researcher progression
instead of an incidental alphabetical order.

<<typer commands>>=
_builtin_tutorials_dir = resources.files("learnlog") / "tutorials"

pytorial.add_typer_subcommand(
    app,
    name="tutorial",
    namespace="learnlog",
    tutorial_paths=(
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "getting-started.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "playback-and-tagging.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "export-and-share.md"),
        str(_builtin_tutorials_dir / "analysing-progsnap2.md"),
    ),
)
@

Like [[play]] in \cref{sec:play}, the interactive execution path
needs a real terminal, so our CLI regression test stays on the
TTY-free [[list]] subcommand.
That is enough to verify the host-side wiring and that the shipped
tutorial resources are visible to pytorial.

The getting-started tutorial should still hand the learner over to a
normal shell step for the first file creation task, so the student opens
[[hello.py]] in a preferred editor from the terminal.
With pytorial's shell hooks available for integrated tutorials, every
shell step after the explicit activation lesson installs
[[eval "$(learnlog activate)"]] as a [[pre_command]].
That keeps the tutorial honest about fresh shells while still making the
project environment available for any extra commands the student tries
before returning from the step.
The run step requires a [[python hello.py]] invocation and a greeting on
stdout.
We express both with [[pytorial]] regex matches rather than plain
[[contains]] strings so that equally valid solutions are not rejected:
the command tolerates [[python3]] and a leading [[./]], and the greeting
tolerates case and punctuation variation around [[hello]] and [[world]].
The final playback step looks for [[learnlog play -b]] (accepting the
long [[--batch]] alternative through a regex), then verifies the
recorded run by matching [[hello.py]] and the same lenient greeting,
plus the stable [[diff --git]] header that proves a real diff was
rendered.
We deliberately drop the [[Script: hello.py]] and
[[stdout: Hello, world!]] line prefixes from the validation: those
strings are an internal output format of [[learnlog play -b]], not
anything the student types, and pinning the tutorial to that exact
format would make it fragile to future viewer changes without
strengthening the learning signal.
We lock the resulting mixed-mode pattern list in with regression tests
against the generated tutorial resource.

Those tests reach a step by its title rather than by its position.  A
tutorial is a document, and a document grows a step in the middle
sooner or later---adding the [[doctor]] step
(\cref{tut:getting-started}) shifted every index after it---so an
assertion keyed on position fails for an edit that is none of its
business, and reports an integer rather than the thing that changed.
Order still carries meaning, so one test spells the sequence out in
full: that keeps the ordering covered while leaving exactly one place
to update when the walkthrough gains a step.

<<test functions>>=
def getting_started_tutorial():
    """Parse the shipped getting-started tutorial resource."""
    from importlib import resources

    from pytorial.catalog import parse_tutorial_text

    tutorial_path = resources.files("learnlog") / "tutorials"
    tutorial_path = tutorial_path / "getting-started.md"
    return parse_tutorial_text(
        tutorial_path.read_text(),
        source_path=tutorial_path,
    )

def getting_started_step(title):
    """Return the shipped getting-started step called ``title``."""
    steps = {
        step.title: step
        for step in getting_started_tutorial().steps
    }
    assert title in steps, sorted(steps)
    return steps[title]

def test_getting_started_keeps_its_step_order():
    """The walkthrough sets up, checks, writes, runs, inspects."""
    assert [
        step.title for step in getting_started_tutorial().steps
    ] == [
        "Create a fresh project",
        "Activate the project environment",
        "Confirm that runs are being recorded",
        "Create a small program",
        "Run the program",
        "Inspect the recorded run",
    ]

def test_tutorial_list_lists_builtin_tutorials():
    """`learnlog tutorial list` includes our shipped tutorials."""
    from learnlog.cli import app
    from typer.testing import CliRunner

    result = CliRunner().invoke(app, ["tutorial", "list"])
    out = result.stdout

    assert result.exit_code == 0
    assert "getting-started" in out
    assert "playback-and-tagging" in out
    assert "export-and-share" in out
    assert "analysing-progsnap2" in out
    assert (
        out.index("getting-started")
        < out.index("playback-and-tagging")
        < out.index("export-and-share")
        < out.index("analysing-progsnap2")
    )

def test_getting_started_uses_shell_step_for_file_creation():
    """File creation stays in the shell, on the project Python."""
    step = getting_started_step("Create a small program")

    assert step.kind is None
    assert step.edit_file is None
    assert step.required_patterns == ()
    assert step.pre_command == 'eval "$(learnlog activate)"\n'
    assert step.check_command is None
    assert "vim hello.py" in step.instructions
    assert "nano hello.py" in step.instructions
    assert "drop to a terminal" in step.instructions
    assert "re-activates the project environment" in step.instructions
    assert 'print("Hello, world!")' in step.instructions

def test_getting_started_reactivates_and_checks_playback():
    """Later shell steps pre-activate the venv and require playback."""
    run_step = getting_started_step("Run the program")
    play_step = getting_started_step("Inspect the recorded run")

    assert run_step.kind is None
    assert run_step.edit_file is None
    assert run_step.pre_command == 'eval "$(learnlog activate)"\n'
    assert tuple(
        (pattern.pattern, pattern.mode)
        for pattern in run_step.required_patterns
    ) == (
        (r"python3?\s+(?:\./)?hello\.py", "regex"),
        (r"(?i)hello[,!\s]*world", "regex"),
    )
    assert run_step.check_command is None
    assert "fresh shell" in run_step.instructions
    assert "re-activated the project environment" in run_step.instructions

    assert play_step.pre_command == 'eval "$(learnlog activate)"\n'
    assert tuple(
        (pattern.pattern, pattern.mode)
        for pattern in play_step.required_patterns
    ) == (
        (r"learnlog\s+play\b[^\n]*(?:-b\b|--batch\b)", "regex"),
        ("hello.py", "contains"),
        (r"(?i)hello[,!\s]*world", "regex"),
        ("diff --git", "contains"),
    )
    assert play_step.check_command is None
    assert "Script: hello.py" in play_step.instructions
    assert "diff" in play_step.instructions
@

One step is keyed to learnlog's \emph{output} rather than to what the
student types.  The [[doctor]] step (\cref{tut:getting-started}) is
satisfied only by a transcript containing the anchored report's
\enquote{Runs of this interpreter are recorded in} line
(\cref{sec:autostart-diagnosis}), which is what makes it a check that
this interpreter really records runs instead of a check that the
student typed the command.  That buys a real verification at the price
of a string shared between a tutorial and a report neither of which
knows about the other: reword the report, and the shipped tutorial
stops being passable---silently, and only for students.

So we drive the two against each other.  We build the report for a
wired interpreter, assemble the transcript the step would record, and
let [[pytorial]]'s own matcher rule on it; the unwired report goes
through the same matcher to confirm that the step gates on the wiring
and not merely on the word [[doctor]] appearing somewhere.

<<test functions>>=
def test_doctor_step_matches_the_report_it_checks(tmp_path):
    """The tutorial's doctor step gates on a wired report."""
    from learnlog.cli import autostart_report
    from pytorial.run import transcript_matches_patterns

    def transcript(**resolver_args):
        _, lines = autostart_report(**resolver_args)
        return "$ learnlog doctor\n" + "\n".join(lines) + "\n"

    purelib = tmp_path / "site-packages"
    purelib.mkdir()
    project = tmp_path / "lab1"
    (project / ".learnlog").mkdir(parents=True)
    marker = purelib / "learnlog_project_root.txt"
    marker.write_text(str(project) + "\n")
    step = getting_started_step(
        "Confirm that runs are being recorded"
    )

    assert transcript_matches_patterns(
        transcript(
            marker_path=marker,
            sys_prefix=str(project / ".venv"),
        ),
        step.required_patterns,
    )
    assert not transcript_matches_patterns(
        transcript(
            marker_path=tmp_path / "bare" / "marker.txt",
            sys_prefix=str(tmp_path / "bare"),
        ),
        step.required_patterns,
    )
