\subsection{The [[push]] command}
\label{sec:push}

The [[push]] command sends the log to [[origin]] with tracking
([[-u]]).
The expected workflow is that the remote repository starts empty, so
a regular push suffices.
For the less common case where the remote already contains commits
(\eg a GitLab repository initialised with a README), the optional
[[--force]] / [[-f]] flag overrides the remote's history.
This is safe because [[.learnlog/]] is a single-writer recording
artefact---there is no risk of overwriting someone else's work.

\paragraph{What \enquote{the log} has to mean.}

[[push]] and [[export]] (\cref{sec:export}) are two ways to hand the
same log to the same teacher, so they have to hand over the same
thing.
They did not.
[[export]] bundles [[--all]], which for [[git bundle]] means every
ref---branches \emph{and} tags---while [[git push --all]] means
[[refs/heads/*]] and nothing else.
A student who marked their milestones with [[learnlog tag lab1]]
(\cref{sec:tag}) and shared by pushing delivered a log in which no
milestone existed, and had no way of noticing: the push reported
success, because sending no tags is not an error.
The same student sharing the same log by [[export]] delivered the
milestones.

We therefore name both ref namespaces explicitly rather than leaning
on a shorthand.
[[--follow-tags]] is the shorthand that looks right and is not: it
sends only \emph{annotated} tags, and [[learnlog tag]] forwards to
[[git tag]], whose default is a lightweight tag---a ref with no tag
object behind it.
Every milestone the shipped workflow creates is exactly the kind
[[--follow-tags]] declines to send, so it would have fixed the
symptom for nobody.
A refspec pair does not ask how the tag was made.

The cost is that a tag the student moved locally ([[git tag -f]]) now
makes the push fail instead of quietly disagreeing with the remote,
which is the same bargain branches have always been on, and
[[--force]] settles both at once.

\paragraph{Inherited standard I/O.}

[[push]] uses [[subprocess.run]] with inherited standard I/O---the
same approach as the [[git]] passthrough command (\cref{sec:git})---so
that the user sees transfer progress and can answer a credential
prompt.
That is also why it stays outside [[LearnlogRepo.git]], even though
the wrapper now covers the byte-stream case that used to push callers
out of it (\cref{sec:git-bytes}).
The wrapper's contract is captured output and a bounded timeout, and
both are wrong here: capturing turns the progress meter and the
password prompt into a buffer nobody sees, and any timeout we could
pick would be a limit on how long the user may take to type a
passphrase or the network to move a repository.
Staying out loses nothing either, which is what separates this case
from the code-state extraction that also thought it had a reason
(\cref{sec:extract-code-state}): Git's own diagnosis has already
reached the terminal, and the exit code below is Git's, so a failed
push is neither silent nor mistaken for a successful one.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command()
def push(
    force: Annotated[
        bool,
        typer.Option(
            "--force", "-f",
            help=(
                "Force-push, overwriting remote history. "
                "Useful when the remote was initialised "
                "with its own commits."
            ),
        ),
    ] = False,
    directory: Annotated[
        pathlib.Path | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--dir", "-C",
            help="Directory containing .learnlog/.",
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Push the learnlog repository to its remote."""
    workdir = resolve_workdir(directory)
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)

    cmd = ["git", "push", "-u"]
    if force:
        cmd.append("--force")
    cmd += ["origin", *PUSH_REFSPECS]

    result = subprocess.run(
        cmd,
        env=repo.env,
        cwd=str(repo.workdir),
    )
    raise typer.Exit(code=result.returncode)
<<constants>>=
PUSH_REFSPECS = (
    "refs/heads/*:refs/heads/*",
    "refs/tags/*:refs/tags/*",
)
@

We test [[push]] by creating a bare repository as the remote target,
configuring [[origin]] to point at it, and verifying that the commits
arrive.
Pushing twice deserves its own test, because it used to fail twice
over.
Finishing the first [[push]] rewrote the very commit it had just sent
(\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}), and even once that was fixed the
push still committed a header and a trailer of its own, so the
sending side moved every time it sent.
Neither happens now---[[push]] is not a recorded run at all
(\cref{sec:veto})---so the second push is an ordinary fast-forward
over whatever the student ran in between, which is what the test
plays out.

