\subsection{The [[clone]] command}
\label{sec:clone}

The [[push]] command delivers a local log to a remote repository, but
we also need the reverse: retrieving a remote log locally.
A teacher cloning a student's pushed learnlog to review it, or a
student moving to a new machine, both need a way to get the
[[.learnlog/]] data from the remote.

The command takes a remote URL, performs a standard [[git clone]] into
a staging directory, moves the resulting [[.git/]] directory to
[[.learnlog/]] in the target working directory, and checks the log's
files out there.
We use a staging directory rather than [[git clone --bare]] because
a standard clone produces a properly configured repository with remote
tracking branches---so subsequent [[push]] and [[pull]]
(\cref{sec:pull}) operations work without additional setup.

Like [[push]], we use [[subprocess.run]] with inherited I/O so the
user sees clone progress and can respond to credential prompts.
We deliberately avoid constructing a [[LearnlogRepo]] before the
clone completes, because its constructor auto-initialises an empty
repository when [[.learnlog/]] is absent.
Once the repository \emph{is} in place, constructing one is the point:
that is what configures it.

The guard that refuses to clone over an existing [[.learnlog/]] is
worth a note, because for most of this command's life it was
unreachable and its refusal was the only thing it ever did.
Cloning into an empty directory is the whole point of [[clone]]---a
teacher fetching a student's pushed log, a student moving machines---
and every invocation used to be a recorded run, so [[initialize]]
had already created the very directory the guard then found
(\cref{sec:veto}).
The command could report nothing but the state it had itself just
produced.
Now that an administrative subcommand leaves no trace, an empty
directory is still empty when the guard looks, and the check finally
means what it says: it fires only when the user really is about to
clone over a log that is already there.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command()
def clone(
    url: Annotated[
        str,
        typer.Argument(
            help="URL of the remote repository to clone.",
        ),
    ],
    directory: Annotated[
        pathlib.Path | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--dir", "-C",
            help=(
                "Target directory for the clone. "
                "Defaults to current directory."
            ),
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Clone a remote learnlog repository into .learnlog/."""
    workdir = pathlib.Path(
        directory or os.getcwd()
    ).resolve()
    learnlog_dir = workdir / ".learnlog"

    <<refuse a target we would have to clone over>>
    <<clone into the target and move the repository into place>>
    <<check the cloned files out into the work tree>>

    typer.echo(f"Cloned into {learnlog_dir}")
@

The target has to be empty, and not merely free of a [[.learnlog/]],
because the clone brings files with it.
A log holds the student's source as it was, and the last thing a clone
may do is write those versions over a directory where somebody is
working: an empty directory makes \enquote{check the log's files out}
a safe operation with nothing to weigh against.
A student who wants their log next to work they already have can clone
into an empty directory and move the [[.learnlog/]] across, which is a
decision they made rather than one we made for them.

