    raise SystemExit(
        f".learnlog/ already exists in {workdir}"
    )
workdir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
if any(workdir.iterdir()):
    raise SystemExit(
        f"{workdir} is not empty. Clone checks the log's "
        f"files out into the target, so it needs an empty "
        f"directory."
    )
@

The clone itself goes into a directory inside the target rather than
into the system temporary directory, and that is what makes it
atomic.
The repository has to end up as [[.learnlog/]] inside the target, and
what moves it there is a rename---which is atomic only within one
filesystem.
[[/tmp]] is routinely a different one, where [[shutil.move]] quietly
degrades into copy-then-delete: an interrupted clone then leaves
behind half a repository, which the guard above reads as a log that is
already there and refuses to replace.
A staging directory beside the destination cannot be on another
filesystem, so [[os.rename]] applies, and either the repository is
there in full or it is not there at all.

Cleaning up catches [[BaseException]] so that [[Ctrl-C]] during a slow
clone is included---an interruption is exactly when a partial
repository would be left---and re-raises, because the user asked for
the command to stop, not for it to report success.

<<clone into the target and move the repository into place>>=
staging = pathlib.Path(
    tempfile.mkdtemp(
        prefix=CLONE_STAGING_PREFIX, dir=workdir,
    )
)
try:
    result = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "clone", url, str(staging)],
        cwd=str(workdir),
    )
    if result.returncode != 0:
        raise SystemExit("Clone failed.")
    os.rename(staging / ".git", learnlog_dir)
except BaseException:
    shutil.rmtree(learnlog_dir, ignore_errors=True)
    raise
finally:
    shutil.rmtree(staging, ignore_errors=True)
<<constants>>=
CLONE_STAGING_PREFIX = ".learnlog-clone-"
@

Moving the repository is where the command used to stop, and what it
left was a work tree with nothing in it.
Git had checked the files out into the staging directory we then
deleted, so every file in the log read as deleted, and the first run
in the clone committed that deletion as the student's work.
Checking them out is therefore not a convenience: it is what makes the
cloned log describe the directory it now lives in.

Constructing the [[LearnlogRepo]] before the checkout does the other
half of the same job.
A cloned repository arrives with none of learnlog's configuration, and
the constructor is what supplies it (\cref{sec:ensure-configured})---
including the exclude file, without which this checkout's own
[[.learnlog/]] would be the first thing the next run staged.

A checkout that fails leaves the log itself intact, so it is reported
rather than cleaned up after: the repository is complete, and the
files can be produced from it at any time with
\mintinline{bash}{learnlog git checkout .}.
Like the bundle writer (\cref{sec:export}), it opts out of [[check]]
so the user gets a sentence instead of a traceback.

A log that holds no files is asked about first, because
\mintinline{bash}{git checkout .} on an empty index is an error and
not a no-op---and cloning a remote that nobody has pushed to yet is
the ordinary way to reach one.
That is a clone with nothing to report, not a clone that went wrong.

<<check the cloned files out into the work tree>>=
repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
if repo.git("ls-files").stdout.strip():
    checkout = repo.git(
        "checkout", ".", check=False,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    if checkout.returncode != 0:
        typer.echo(
            f"Cloned, but could not check out the files: "
            f"{checkout.stderr.strip()}",
            err=True,
        )
@

