\subsection{The [[pull]] command}
\label{sec:pull}

The [[pull]] command is the receiving counterpart to [[push]]: it
brings the commits another machine recorded into this copy of the
log.

The obvious implementation is [[git pull]] under the learnlog
environment, and it is the wrong one.
That environment is [[--git-dir=.learnlog --work-tree=.]], so the
work tree Git is asked to update \emph{is the student's project
directory}, and the index it merges into is the shared index the runs
commit from.
Merging there fails in three separate ways, and all of them cost the
student work rather than merely returning an error:

\begin{description}
\item[A merge that succeeds] writes the incoming files over the
  student's source.
  Synchronising a log has no business rewriting the program the log is
  about, and an edit overwritten this way was never committed
  anywhere, so nothing can bring it back.
\item[A merge that races a run] checks the merged files out first and
  moves the ref last.
  A run that commits in between takes the ref, the update fails with
  [[cannot lock ref 'HEAD']], and the merged files are left in the
  work tree belonging to no commit at all---so the next run commits
  them as the student's own work.
\item[A merge that conflicts] leaves [[MERGE_HEAD]] and an unmerged
  shared index behind in [[.learnlog/]].
  The next run's [[git commit]] reads both as its own: it consummates
  somebody else's half-finished merge, and records a code state full
  of conflict markers.
\end{description}

The three share one cause---the merge machinery writes through the
work tree and the shared index, and both of those belong to the
runs---so we stop using it.
[[pull]] writes exactly two kinds of thing: objects, which no reader
can reach until a ref names them, and one branch ref, moved under the
commit lock.
It never writes the work tree, never writes the shared index, and
never leaves state in [[.learnlog/]] for a run to trip over.
Everything below follows from that rule.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command()
def pull(
    directory: Annotated[
        pathlib.Path | None,
        typer.Option(
            "--dir", "-C",
            help="Directory containing .learnlog/.",
        ),
    ] = None,
):
    """Pull new commits from the remote."""
    workdir = resolve_workdir(directory)
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    <<fetch the remote branches>>
    <<find the local and remote tips>>
    <<decide the new tip, or stop at a conflict>>
    <<advance the branch under the commit lock>>
@

\paragraph{Fetching.}
Fetching is the only part that talks to the network, so it keeps
inherited standard I/O for transfer progress and credential prompts,
for the reasons spelled out for [[push]] (\cref{sec:push}).
It is also the only part that could not damage anything even if we
wanted it to: [[git fetch]] writes remote-tracking refs and objects,
and touches neither the work tree nor the index.
A missing or unreachable [[origin]] surfaces here, and its exit code
is the one worth reporting, so we stop on it.

<<fetch the remote branches>>=
fetched = subprocess.run(
    ["git", "fetch", "origin"],
    env=repo.env,
    cwd=str(repo.workdir),
)
if fetched.returncode != 0:
    raise typer.Exit(code=fetched.returncode)
@

\paragraph{Which branch.}
[[git pull --all]] updated every branch it could reach; we update the
branch the log is on.
That is a description of the old behaviour rather than a reduction of
it: [[.learnlog/]] is created with one branch and only ever grows
commits on that branch, so the current branch \emph{is} the log.
A remote that has no such branch is not an error---a teacher may have
created the destination repository moments ago---so we say so and
stop.

<<find the local and remote tips>>=
head = repo.git(
    "symbolic-ref", "--short", "HEAD", check=False,
)
if head.returncode != 0:
    raise SystemExit(
        "The log is not on a branch; cannot pull."
    )
branch = head.stdout.strip()
remote = repo.git(
    "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet",
    f"refs/remotes/origin/{branch}", check=False,
)
if remote.returncode != 0:
    typer.echo(
        f"origin has no {branch} branch; nothing to pull."
    )
    return
remote_tip = remote.stdout.strip()
local_tip = repo.git(
    "rev-parse", "--verify", "HEAD",
).stdout.strip()
@

\paragraph{Three cases, one of which needs a merge.}
When the remote tip is already an ancestor of ours there is nothing to
bring in.
When ours is an ancestor of the remote's, the histories have not
diverged and the new tip is simply the remote's---no merge, no tree
computation, one [[update-ref]].
That fast-forward is the common case, because the usual shape is one
machine recording and another reading, and it is worth recognising
explicitly rather than letting a merge discover it.

<<decide the new tip, or stop at a conflict>>=
if is_ancestor(repo, remote_tip, local_tip):
    typer.echo("Already up to date.")
    return
if is_ancestor(repo, local_tip, remote_tip):
    new_tip = remote_tip
    summary = f"Fast-forwarded {branch} to origin."
else:
    new_tip, conflicts = merge_log_histories(
        repo, local_tip, remote_tip,
    )
    if new_tip is None:
        raise SystemExit(describe_pull_conflict(conflicts))
    summary = f"Merged origin into {branch}."
@

<<helper functions>>=
def is_ancestor(repo, candidate, descendant):
    """Return whether ``candidate`` is an ancestor of ``descendant``.

    Asks Git the question with ``merge-base --is-ancestor``, whose
    exit status is the answer.
    """
    probe = repo.git(
        "merge-base", "--is-ancestor",
        candidate, descendant, check=False,
    )
    return probe.returncode == 0
@

\paragraph{Advancing the branch.}
Everything above is either a read or a write of objects nobody can
reach yet.
The single write that makes a pull real is the branch update, and it
is the one that must not interleave with a run's commit, so it happens
under the same [[flock]] the run commits take
(\cref{sec:commit-lock}).
The lock cannot cover the merge as well---computing a merge over a
large tree takes long enough that holding the lock would block the
student's next program from starting---so we hand [[update-ref]] the
tip we merged from as the expected old value.
Git then refuses the update if a run moved the branch while we were
merging, which turns that window into a retry instead of a lost
commit.

<<advance the branch under the commit lock>>=
with repo.commit_lock():
    advanced = repo.git(
        "update-ref", f"refs/heads/{branch}",
        new_tip, local_tip, check=False,
    )
if advanced.returncode != 0:
    raise SystemExit(
        "The log moved while pulling; nothing was changed. "
        "Run learnlog pull again."
    )
typer.echo(summary)
@

\paragraph{Merging without a work tree.}
A three-way merge needs somewhere to put intermediate state, and the
whole point of this redesign is that the student's directory is not
it.
Git offers two ways out, and which one is available depends on the
version installed.
[[git merge-tree --write-tree]] (Git 2.38 and later) performs a full
merge---rename detection, content merging, the lot---entirely in the
object database, and prints the object name of the merged tree.
Older versions have no such command, so we fall back to a three-way
[[read-tree]] into a private index file, which resolves everything
that can be resolved by path and reports the rest as a conflict.
The fallback is weaker: it cannot merge two edits inside one file, so
it declares a conflict where the newer command would have succeeded.
That is the right way to be wrong here.
Refusing to merge leaves both logs intact and tells the student to ask
for help; the alternative---running the content merge in a checkout
somewhere---brings back the work tree we just removed from the design.

Either way the result is a tree, and a tree is not yet history: we
turn it into a merge commit with [[commit-tree]], whose two parents
record that these two logs became one.
The message travels on standard input, as every commit message in
learnlog does (\cref{sec:commit-message-stdin}).

<<helper functions>>=
def merge_log_histories(repo, ours, theirs):
    """Merge two diverged log histories.

    Returns a ``(commit, conflicts)`` pair.  On success ``commit`` is
    the object name of a new merge commit and ``conflicts`` is empty;
    otherwise ``commit`` is ``None`` and ``conflicts`` lists the paths
    Git could not merge.
    """
    if git_merges_trees(repo):
        tree, conflicts = merge_trees_directly(
            repo, ours, theirs,
        )
    else:
        tree, conflicts = merge_trees_in_private_index(
            repo, ours, theirs,
        )
    if tree is None:
        return None, conflicts
    merged = repo.git(
        "commit-tree", tree, "-p", ours, "-p", theirs,
        input=PULL_MERGE_MESSAGE,
        timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    return merged.stdout.strip(), []
<<constants>>=
PULL_MERGE_MESSAGE = "learnlog: merge origin into the local log\n"
@

We ask [[git --version]] rather than probing [[merge-tree]] itself
with a trial merge, because a probe would have to invent two commits
to merge and would then have to tell \enquote{this Git cannot} apart
from \enquote{those commits conflict}---which is the very distinction
the caller needs.

<<helper functions>>=
def git_merges_trees(repo):
    """Return whether ``git merge-tree --write-tree`` exists."""
    probe = repo.git("--version", check=False)
    found = GIT_VERSION_PATTERN.search(probe.stdout)
    if found is None:
        return False
    version = (int(found.group(1)), int(found.group(2)))
    return version >= MERGE_TREE_VERSION
<<constants>>=
GIT_VERSION_PATTERN = re.compile(r"(\d+)\.(\d+)")
MERGE_TREE_VERSION = (2, 38)
@

[[merge-tree]] reports a conflict with exit status~1 and still prints
the (conflicted) tree, so the exit status alone does not separate
\enquote{conflict} from \enquote{that is not a commit}: both are 1.
What separates them is the output---a real merge always prints a tree
on the first line, a rejected argument prints nothing---so that is
what we test.
With [[--name-only]] the lines after the tree are the conflicted
paths, terminated by a blank line before Git's human-readable
commentary.

<<helper functions>>=
def merge_trees_directly(repo, ours, theirs):
    """Merge two commits with ``git merge-tree``.

    Needs no work tree and no index: the merged tree is written
    straight to the object database.
    """
    merged = repo.git(
        "merge-tree", "--write-tree", "--name-only",
        ours, theirs, check=False, timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    )
    lines = merged.stdout.splitlines()
    if not lines:
        raise SystemExit(
            f"Merging the logs failed: {merged.stderr.strip()}"
        )
    if merged.returncode == 0:
        return lines[0].strip(), []
    conflicts = []
    for line in lines[1:]:
        if not line.strip():
            break
        conflicts.append(line.strip())
    return None, conflicts
@

The fallback merges into a file that is not the shared index, using
the same [[run_index]] machinery a run uses to keep its staging out of
everyone else's way (\cref{gitrepo}).
[[read-tree -i]] is what keeps this honest: it tells Git not to
compare against the work tree, which is both what makes the merge
possible with a dirty project directory and what guarantees no file is
written.
[[--aggressive]] resolves the cases a three-way index merge can decide
on its own---one side changed a path, both sides made the same change,
both sides deleted it---and leaves the rest as unmerged stages, which
is exactly what [[write-tree]] refuses to turn into a tree.

<<helper functions>>=
def merge_trees_in_private_index(repo, ours, theirs):
    """Merge two commits in a private index file.

    The path taken when Git is too old for ``merge-tree``; resolves
    per-path changes only, and reports anything else as a conflict.
    """
    base = repo.git("merge-base", ours, theirs, check=False)
    if base.returncode != 0:
        return None, []
    with repo.run_index(PULL_MERGE_INDEX):
        read = repo.git(
            "read-tree", "-m", "-i", "--aggressive",
            base.stdout.strip(), ours, theirs,
            check=False, timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
        )
        if read.returncode != 0:
            raise SystemExit(
                f"Merging the logs failed: {read.stderr.strip()}"
            )
        written = repo.git(
            "write-tree", check=False,
            timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
        )
        if written.returncode == 0:
            return written.stdout.strip(), []
        unmerged = repo.git(
            "ls-files", "--unmerged", check=False,
        )
    return None, sorted({
        line.split("\t", 1)[1]
        for line in unmerged.stdout.splitlines()
        if "\t" in line
    })
<<constants>>=
PULL_MERGE_INDEX = "pull-merge"
@

A conflict is where a student most needs to be told that nothing
happened, because \enquote{it failed} usually means \enquote{something
was half-done}.
Here it does not, and the message says so before it says what to try
next.

<<helper functions>>=
def describe_pull_conflict(conflicts):
    """Explain why a pull stopped and that nothing changed."""
    if conflicts:
        reason = "the merge conflicts in " + ", ".join(conflicts)
    else:
        reason = "they share no common commit to merge from"
    return (
        "Pull stopped: the local log and origin have diverged and "
        f"{reason}.  Nothing was changed: your files and your local "
        "log are exactly as they were.  Ask your teacher for help, "
        "or use learnlog clone to fetch the remote log into a fresh "
        "directory."
    )
@

We test [[pull]] by pushing from the original repository, cloning
into a second directory, adding a new commit to the original and
pushing again, then pulling in the clone.
The clone should see the new commit after the pull:

<<test functions>>=
def test_pull_after_clone(workdir, monkeypatch):
