\subsection{The [[tag]] command}
\label{sec:tag}

While [[learnlog git tag lab1]] already works via the
[[git]] passthrough, typing [[learnlog tag lab1]] is more
discoverable---students see it in [[learnlog --help]]
alongside [[play]] and [[export]], which reinforces that
tagging is a first-class operation rather than a Git
implementation detail.

The implementation mirrors the [[git]] passthrough
(\cref{sec:git}): we find the [[.learnlog/]] directory,
set the Git environment, and forward all arguments to
[[git tag]].

[[git tag]] with no ref tags HEAD, and which commit that is depends
on something outside this command: whether [[learnlog tag]] is itself
recorded.
It is not (\cref{sec:veto}), and that is what makes the shipped
workflow work.
The tag lands on the last thing the student ran, so [[learnlog play
lab1]] starts where they think they left off.
While administrative commands still logged themselves, HEAD at this
moment was the header commit of the [[learnlog tag]] run---so the tag
named the act of tagging, on a commit that every view filters out.

<<typer commands>>=
@app.command(
    name="tag",
    context_settings={
        "allow_extra_args": True,
        "allow_interspersed_args": False,
        "ignore_unknown_options": True,
    },
)
def tag_command(
    ctx: typer.Context,
):
    """Manage tags on the learnlog repository.

    All arguments are forwarded to ``git tag``,
    configured to operate on the ``.learnlog/``
    directory.  For example, ``learnlog tag lab1``
    creates a tag named ``lab1`` on the current HEAD.
    """
    workdir = find_learnlog_dir()
    if workdir is None:
        raise SystemExit(
            "No .learnlog/ directory found."
        )

    learnlog_dir = workdir / ".learnlog"
    env = git_environment(learnlog_dir, workdir)

    result = subprocess.run(
        ["git", "tag"] + ctx.args,
        env=env,
        cwd=str(workdir),
